    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d375">

                <pb facs="0379" n="263" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg263"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 339.41/5.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Chargé d’Affaires
                        to the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">British Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-06-17">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 17, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I beg to thank you on Sir
                    Cecil Spring Rice’s behalf for your letter to him of June 2, in which you are
                    good enough to communicate the substance of instructions issued to the U. S. S.
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Castine</hi> for the protection of foreign life and
                    property in the Dominican Republic.* * *</p>
                <p>Sir Edward Grey desires me to say that His Majesty’s Government trust that
                    instructions to protect British life and property in case of need may be given
                    to whatever vessel of war the United States Government may decide to send to
                    Samana Bay. You will greatly oblige me if with your usual courtesy you could
                    communicate in this sense with the Navy Department.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Colville Barclay</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
